			label "genre choice"
			choice
				`	"How long is it?"`
					goto "book length"
				`	"Why is it a story about humanity stealing from the Quarg?"`
					goto "quarg"
				`	"That flap has a very nice illustration."`
					goto "illustration"
				`	"Anything else aside from Beyond the Hyperdrive?"`
			label "other novels"
			`	"Beyond the Hyperdrive is the fifth book I've ever published, actually," says Anna. "The other four never... they didn't do very well." She glances at the ground. "I also write short stories on the hypernet in my free time. In fact, I'll probably start writing my sixth in a couple months, as soon as I find a good idea."`
			choice
				`	"What kind of short stories have you written?"`
					goto "short stories"
				`	"How about your previous books?"`
			label "previous books"
			`	"They weren't really successful. To be honest, I'm still struggling to make ends meet. I make a little money off of the few people that buy them, but I never quite reach the five-digits. The first one was self-published. All the publishers rejected it, so I couldn't get much attention for it. The second one was about a kid from the Paradise Worlds, sent to the Dirt Belt by his parents for being spoiled. The third one was about that same kid being kidnapped by pirates. I stopped that series since it was clear it would never sell. And, the fourth one, well..." Anna sighs. "That one was such a failure I don't even want to tell you about it.`
			`	"Beyond the Hyperdrive is the fifth one, and I'm hoping it will sell better than the previous four."`
			choice
				`	"How about short stories?"`
			label "short stories"
			`	"I write short stories and put them up on the hypernet sometimes. Helps me improve my writing and get that urge satiated without committing to a project as long-term as a novel."`
			`	Anna shows you a webpage with all her stories. The view counts appear to be in the hundreds, with the occasional one reaching four digits.`
			choice
				`	"So why is Beyond the Hyperdrive about humans stealing from the Quarg?"`
					goto "quarg"
			label "illustration"
			`	"Well, thank you! I got it printed in a shop on Starcross - the shipping fees were exorbitant, but it's definitely very nice!"`
				goto "genre choice"
			label "book length"
			`	"About three hundred pages - ninety thousand words. Not too long."`
			choice
				`	"Have you written any other novels?"`
					goto "other novels"
				`	"What made you decide to set the Quarg as villains in Beyond the Hyperdrive?"`
			label "quarg"
			`	Over the course of a few seconds, Anna's optimistic smile sinks, and her eyes seem to dull. "You'll probably laugh, but, well... I have something of a... problem with them. I still remember when I was a little girl, my parents took me to Humanika during a vacation trip. It was my first time on a world so alien, so naturally, I wanted to explore. I wandered off from our tour group, and soon realized I was lost." She looks back at you momentarily, as if gauging how you're taking her tale. "There were so many hallways, twists and turns - I still cannot figure out how I ended up in that maze. It was a darker portion of the spaceport, and as I kept calling out for my parents, I only ever heard my own voice echoing back. And then, I saw it. A large Quarg, with eyes of a deep, piercing purple, staring right at me, far down the hallway. I broke into a sprint to flee, but I kept running into more and more Quarg... I don't remember much after that, just that I eventually got back to my parents.`
			`	"And then when I grew up, when I was a teenager, I decided to do a project on the Quarg for college, assisting a journalist writing an article on them. I tried to keep the memories of that episode in my childhood away, for the sake of the project, but as I interviewed them, as I tried to talk and befriend them... even the polite ones, they seemed so... aloof. Different - and not in a good way. I just felt a great unease being there, near them again. It certainly didn't help that they often responded with silence to my questions, as if they had something to hide. For months I tried, but they just would not budge, and that eerie feeling whenever one was with me never went away..."`
			label priorchoice
			choice
				`	(Tell her about the Coalition.)`
					goto coalition
				`	"That must have been rough, stuck on Humanika. How did your parents eventually find you?"`
					goto parents
				`	"Can I have a copy of the book?"`
					to display
						not "beyond the hyperdrive - asked for book copy"
				`	"Well that's all well and good. But I have my own business to attend to now. Good luck with your book, thanks for your time, and goodbye."`
					decline
			action
				set "beyond the hyperdrive - asked for book copy"
			`	"I'm afraid I can't afford to give it away right now."`
				goto priorchoice
			label parents
			`	"I have no idea. But it was distressing being stuck with that Quarg for what felt like an eternity. I don't remember exactly how long. Eventually, another Quarg brought my parents to me. I told them I wanted to get off Humanika as fast as possible and get back to Zug. They obliged, and we left that world; I've never wanted to go back."`
			choice
				`	"So you said your books don't sell well, right?"`
			`	While Anna tries her best to maintain her composure, her features clearly show she's not comfortable with the topic. "No, they don't," she eventually says. "Even some of my friends and family don't have much good to say about them, and fewer still are inclined to do anything for me." Her eyes take on a vaguely more optimistic light. "Do you, perhaps... work for some publisher? Someone interested in my work?"`
			choice
				`	(Tell her about the Coalition.)`
				`	"No actually, I'm afraid I don't know anyone who could help you."`
					decline
			label coalition
			`	You begin explaining the Coalition to Anna, going in-depth about their culture and their long history of opposing the Quarg, seeing as Anna has less than positive opinions of them. At first Anna does not believe you, but you reassure her it's real. You also mention the population of the Coalition and the novelty of an "alien" author coming into the area.`
			`	"Will they really like my books?"`
			choice
				`	"They will, hopefully."`
				`	"Well, it's better to try than to just give up."`
			`	"These stories might just inspire my next novel. But they wouldn't speak our language. How are they going to read my books?" You reply, "They have a translation device."`
			`	"That's reassuring... but, how am I even going to get there?" she queries.`
			choice
				`	"I'll take you there."`
				`	"I don't think you can."`
					goto decline
			`	Her tone of voice becomes much more excited. "Do I have to pay anything? I can't afford off-world transport at all, let alone to a completely different segment of the galaxy! Actually, I know - I'll give you a copy of Beyond the Hyperdrive! Thank you so much! Just wait for a while. I'll go over to my house and bring what I'll need for the trip, and then we can go to whatever this Coalition thing is," says Anna.`
			`	As you wait for Anna, you look over your maps of Coalition space to double-check what planet the Coalition book club is on. After a quick look, you find <destination>.`
			`	After approximately an hour of waiting, Anna appears outside of your ship, knocking on the hatch. You open it to let her inside and prepare to leave the spaceport.`
				accept
			label decline
			`	Filled with disappointment, Anna says, "Well, I'm afraid I have other things to do, then. Thank you for the stories, at least." She leaves.`
				decline
			label bookcopy
			action
				set "beyond the hyperdrive - asked for book copy"
			`	"I'm afraid I can't afford to give one away right now, but I'm happy to show it to you. I assume you already know that it's the story of a group of humans who steal the Quarg's jumping technology and see what's truly beyond the hyperlanes we've known for so long."`
				goto "beyond the hyperdrive description"
	on accept
		clear "beyond the hyperdrive - asked for book copy"
	on decline
		clear "beyond the hyperdrive - asked for book copy"
	on enter
		system
			government "Coalition" "Heliarch"
		conversation
			`As the flames of your jump drive fade away, Anna comes out of her bunk in shock. "What was that? It felt like the whole ship vanished out of our space altogether! Where are we?"`
			choice
				`	"My jump drive would like to welcome you to Coalition space."`
				`	"That was a jump drive."`
				`	"That's what happens every time I go to Coalition space with a jump drive."`
			`	"Wow. I can't believe this is happening!" Anna looks out the window, then turns back.`
			`	"Can I take a look at your jump drive? I'm interested to see how it works," Anna says. You take her to the engine room of the <ship>, where she begins taking notes and pictures of the drive.`
			`	"Well, thank you for bringing me here," Anna says when she finishes. "I didn't sleep too well, so I'll see you later. Goodbye!" She heads back into her cabin, and you settle back into the controls.`
	on complete
		set "coalition: struggling author - beyond the hyperdrive"
		conversation
			`Anna gives you the book before you both exit the <ship>. The moment you are out, three Kimek with golden circlets on their heads approach you and Anna.`
			`	"Greetings, <first> <last>. This other human you have brought, who she is, may I ask you?"`
			`	"Oh, I write science fiction novels," replies Anna, only mildly taken aback by the sight of giant beetles.`
			`	The Kimek begin conversing amongst themselves in their native language for a good ten to twenty seconds before they move to face you again.`
			`	Then, switching the translation box back on, the Kimek explains that Anna is welcome to be with you, but reminds you that she will not be allowed to do much until she registers with the Heliarchs on the Ring of Friendship.`
			`	"Okay," says Anna, "I'll explore the spaceport a little bit to find some inspiration. Get your ship ready and meet me there."`

